## ⌨️ 2.4 - 2.7 Writing Event-Driven Code
GUI applications are **event-driven**, meaning they wait for and respond to specific actions performed by the user while the application runs (such as clicking a button or moving the mouse).

### 1. Displaying a Message Box (MessageBox)
```csharp
private void messageButton_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    // Displays a small pop-up dialog box containing the text
    MessageBox.Show("Hello World"); 
}
```

### 2. Changing Label Text (Label Output)
```csharp
private void showAnswerButton_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    // Assigns a new text string value to the Label control
    answerLabel.Text = "Jamhuuriya University"; 
}
```

### 3. Showing and Hiding Images (PictureBox Visibility)
Statements in code execute in a sequential order (**Sequential Execution**). This means each line runs exactly in the order it appears from top to bottom:
```csharp
private void showBackButton_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    cardBackPictureBox.Visible = true;  // Makes the card back image visible
    cardFacePictureBox.Visible = false; // Hides the card face image
}
```

